## [ISSUE] Blacklist and duplicate checks are case-sensitive

**Severity:** MEDIUM — `BLACKLISTED_GUEST_EMAILS` can be bypassed by changing letter case.

### The Problem
```ts
const blacklistedGuestEmails = process.env.BLACKLISTED_GUEST_EMAILS
  ? process.env.BLACKLISTED_GUEST_EMAILS.split(",").map((email) => email.toLowerCase())
  : [];
const uniqueGuests = guests.filter(
  (guest) =>
    !booking.attendees.some((attendee) => guest === attendee.email) &&
    !blacklistedGuestEmails.includes(guest)
);
```
The list is lowercased but `guest` is not.

### Theory / Proof
With `BLACKLISTED_GUEST_EMAILS=spam@x.com`, the guest `Spam@x.com` gives `["spam@x.com"].includes("Spam@x.com") === false`, so it is added and emailed. `z.string().email()` does not normalise case. Mail domains are case-insensitive, and in practice mailbox local parts are too, so this reaches the blocked mailbox. The existing flow does it right: `handleNewBooking.ts:742-744` lowercases the guest (and strips `+` aliases via `extractBaseEmail`) before comparing.

### Research
RFC 5321 §2.4: domain names are case-insensitive; nearly all providers treat local parts case-insensitively.

### Recommendation
```ts
const uniqueGuests = guests.filter((guest) => {
  const normalized = extractBaseEmail(guest).toLowerCase();
  return (
    !booking.attendees.some((a) => a.email.toLowerCase() === guest.toLowerCase()) &&
    !blacklistedGuestEmails.includes(normalized)
  );
});
```

## [GAP ANALYSIS] Add-guests emails ignore the event type's "disable standard emails" settings

**Severity:** MEDIUM — organizers who turned off Cal.com's standard emails (e.g. to send their own via workflows) still get these sent to hosts and attendees.

### The Problem
```ts
export const sendAddGuestsEmails = async (calEvent: CalendarEvent, newGuests: string[]) => {
  ...
  emailsToSend.push(sendEmail(() => new OrganizerAddGuestsEmail({ calEvent: calendarEvent })));
```
It has no `eventTypeMetadata` parameter and sends unconditionally.

### Theory / Proof
The sibling function directly above (`email-manager.ts:495-524`, `sendLocationChangeEmails`) takes `eventTypeMetadata` and wraps host emails in `if (!eventTypeDisableHostEmail(eventTypeMetadata))` and attendee emails in `if (!eventTypeDisableAttendeeEmail(eventTypeMetadata))`. `editLocation.handler.ts` passes `booking.eventType?.metadata`. `addGuests.handler.ts:168` passes nothing.

### Recommendation
```ts
export const sendAddGuestsEmails = async (calEvent: CalendarEvent, newGuests: string[], eventTypeMetadata?: EventTypeMetadata) => {
  ...
  if (!eventTypeDisableHostEmail(eventTypeMetadata)) { /* organizer + team member emails */ }
  if (!eventTypeDisableAttendeeEmail(eventTypeMetadata)) { /* attendee emails */ }
};
// handler
await sendAddGuestsEmails(evt, uniqueGuests, booking.eventType?.metadata as EventTypeMetadata);
```

## [GAP ANALYSIS] No booking status check before adding guests

**Severity:** MEDIUM — cancelled or rejected bookings can be "revived" in new guests' calendars.

### The Problem
After `if (!booking) throw ...` the handler goes straight to permission checks and `booking.update`. There is no check on `booking.status` or `booking.endTime`.

### Theory / Proof
The UI only shows the "Edit" menu for upcoming, non-cancelled bookings (`BookingListItem.tsx:544`, `isUpcoming && !isCancelled`), but the API is callable directly with any `bookingId`. For a CANCELLED booking, the emails built in `attendee-add-guests-email.ts` / `organizer-add-guests-email.ts` hard-code `status: "CONFIRMED"` and `method: "REQUEST"`, so every attendee receives an ICS that marks the cancelled meeting as confirmed.

### Recommendation
```ts
if (booking.status === BookingStatus.CANCELLED || booking.status === BookingStatus.REJECTED) {
  throw new TRPCError({ code: "BAD_REQUEST", message: "booking_is_cancelled" });
}
if (dayjs(booking.endTime).isBefore(dayjs())) {
  throw new TRPCError({ code: "BAD_REQUEST", message: "booking_in_past" });
}
```

## [ISSUE] Error toast fallback never used

**Severity:** LOW — users can see `undefined: Failed to fetch` instead of the localized "Unable to add guests".

### The Problem
```ts
const message = `${err.data?.code}: ${t(err.message)}`;
showToast(message || t("unable_to_add_guests"), "error");
```
A template literal is never an empty string (it always contains `": "`), so `|| t("unable_to_add_guests")` never runs. When `err.data` is undefined (network error), the toast reads `undefined: ...`.

### Recommendation
```ts
onError: (err) => {
  const message = err.data?.code ? `${err.data.code}: ${t(err.message)}` : t("unable_to_add_guests");
  showToast(message, "error");
},
```
